---

The most important part: Trap Questions

For Module 1.1, I would maintain a dedicated "Java Trap Bank".

Examples:

Trap 1

> Is JVM platform independent?



Expected reasoning: No. Java bytecode is portable; the JVM implementation is platform-specific.

Trap 2

> Does javac compile Java directly into machine code?



No. It produces JVM bytecode.

Trap 3

> Does loading a class always mean its static initializer has executed?



No. Class loading and initialization are distinct lifecycle concepts.

Trap 4

> If a class is public, can every module access it?



No. JPMS readability/exports can still restrict access.

Trap 5

> If an application compiled successfully, is runtime execution guaranteed?



No. Runtime dependency, classpath, JVM version, module and environment problems can still cause failure.

Trap 6

> If the same class exists in two JARs, Java automatically chooses the correct one.



Dangerous assumption. Classpath ordering/class-loader behavior can determine which class is resolved.

Trap 7

> Java is interpreted.



Incomplete. Modern JVM execution can involve interpretation and JIT compilation.

Trap 8

> JDK 21 means the application can always run on Java 17.



No. Bytecode/API/language compatibility must be considered.


---

Module 1.1 Mastery Gate

Don't consider this module complete until you can answer, without notes, questions such as:

> What exactly happens when I type java MyApp?



and:

> What exactly happens when I type javac MyApp.java?



and:

> What happens between .class bytecode and CPU execution?



and:

> Why did my application compile successfully but fail with NoClassDefFoundError?



and:

> Why does a newer .class file fail on an older JVM?



and:

> Classpath vs module path — why do both exist?



and:

> How would I diagnose a JVM problem in production?



Your original mastery gate expects precisely this progression: manual compilation, bytecode inspection, classpath debugging, JPMS, compatibility diagnosis, JIT understanding and JVM diagnostics. 


---

Final assessment structure

For every future module, I'll maintain the same structure:

MODULE
│
├── 1. What is it? → Basic
├── 2. Why? → Basic/Conceptual
├── 3. How? → Intermediate
├── 4. Internals → Advanced
├── 5. Theory → Rules
├── 6. Implementation → Coding
├── 7. Output → Prediction
├── 8. Traps → Misconceptions
├── 9. Debugging → Broken code
├── 10. Performance → Runtime
├── 11. Production → Real systems
├── 12. Design → Architecture
└── 13. Senior → Interview/decision making

This will turn your existing syllabus into a full mastery question bank, rather than just a study-notes document. Your existing modules already use the same underlying mastery philosophy—explain, implement, understand internals, handle edge cases, debug and apply in production. 

Next in sequence: Module 1.2 — Language Fundamentals, covering every one of its 26 sections with this same depth.
